    "result_text": "The only concrete test class is `AcceptanceTest` (the other two Java files are the abstract base and the audit-log helper), and it passes. The change is complete and the build is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nReplaced the string `membershipTier` with a numeric `membershipLevel` (1\u20133), computed on creation.\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 Replaced the `membershipTier` (string, `SILVER`/`BRONZE`/`GOLD`) response property with `membershipLevel` (integer, `minimum: 1`, `maximum: 3`), regenerating the `OwnerDto`. This removes the tier field from the response in favour of the level.\n\n2. **`src/main/java/.../mapper/OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 Changed the derivation to compute the level:\n   ```java\n   Math.min(3, 1\n       + (email present ? 1 : 0)\n       + (namesakeCount == 0 ? 1 : 0))\n   ```\n   Starts at 1, +1 for a present email, +1 when `namesakeCount` is 0, capped at 3 (leaving level 4 for tenure). The old GOLD-from-`householdSize` logic is gone.\n\n3. **`src/main/java/.../owner/CountHouseholdMembers.java`** \u2014 Removed the now-stale Javadoc line claiming the mapper derives a GOLD tier from household size.\n\n**On the audit line:** The task says \"anything that recorded the tier \u2026 must record the level instead.\" I checked `AuditOwnerCreated` (and grepped the whole codebase for `tier`) \u2014 the create audit line records `id`, `customerCode`, and `registrationDate`, and no code path recorded the tier. So there was nothing recording the tier to convert; the only tier consumer was the response mapper, which now emits the level.\n\n**Verification:** `AcceptanceTest.coreNumericLevel` (unique owner with email \u2192 `1 + email + namesake0 = 3`) passes, and the full `mvn test` run is `BUILD SUCCESS`.",
